WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:19.443
In the three years since the death of Sherlock Holmes, my dear wife had also died.

2
00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:24.625
Thanks to my friendship with the great detective, I continued to be interested in crime.

3
00:00:25.746 --> 00:00:30.648
For my own amusement and to distract myself from my grief, I often followed interesting cases.

4
00:00:31.416 --> 00:00:34.037
But of course I was never successful in solving any crimes.

5
00:00:34.898 --> 00:00:40.160
I think that Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard also missed Holmes because he sometimes visited me to discuss

6
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.060
his latest cases.

7
00:00:42.021 --> 00:00:47.743
One spring evening our topic of conversation was Ronald Adair's murder, or as the newspapers called it, the

8
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:48.684
Park Lane mystery.

9
00:00:49.724 --> 00:00:53.366
Everyone in London was talking about this case, which was baffling the police.

10
00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:58.108
"There's no question that Adair was murdered," Lestrade said as we sat in my study.

11
00:01:00.184 --> 00:01:02.546
"But I can't figure out how it was done or who did it."

12
00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:07.649
"Perhaps it would help if you went over the details of the case, starting with what you know

13
00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:08.349
about the victim."

14
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.911
I poured a cup of tea for each of us.

15
00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:12.272
"That's what Mr.

16
00:01:12.312 --> 00:01:12.912
Holmes would do."

17
00:01:14.013 --> 00:01:14.814
Lestrade nodded.

18
00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:19.997
"Ronald Adair was a wealthy young man who spent his time with the best members of society.

19
00:01:20.958 --> 00:01:25.921
One thing that makes this case so puzzling is that he didn't seem to have any enemies—male or

20
00:01:26.021 --> 00:01:28.042
female. Everyone liked him."

21
00:01:29.023 --> 00:01:30.728
"That is unusual," I said.

22
00:01:30.748 --> 00:01:31.909
"Go on."

23
00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:36.311
"Adair belonged to three London card clubs, where he regularly played whist."

24
00:01:37.151 --> 00:01:39.772
Lestrade explained that whist is a game played with a partner.

25
00:01:41.873 --> 00:01:47.736
On the night Adair died, he and his whist partner, Colonel Sebastian Moran, played against Daniel Murray and

26
00:01:47.856 --> 00:01:48.736
Sir John Hardy.

27
00:01:49.597 --> 00:01:52.878
All three men said that Adair never lost much money at cards.

28
00:01:52.898 --> 00:01:57.120
"I remember reading that in the newspaper stories about the murder," I said.

29
00:01:58.164 --> 00:02:02.446
"I also read that Adair and Colonel Moran had recently won a large amount of money while playing

30
00:02:02.486 --> 00:02:04.988
cards. Now tell me about Adair's death."

31
00:02:05.928 --> 00:02:10.131
"After playing cards Adair returned home at around ten o'clock," Lestrade said.

32
00:02:10.151 --> 00:02:15.073
"His mother and sister, with whom he lived on Park Lane, were out visiting relatives.

33
00:02:16.074 --> 00:02:19.736
Anticipating his arrival, the maid had lit a fire in the fireplace in his study.

34
00:02:20.536 --> 00:02:22.717
The room is on the second floor at the front of the house.

35
00:02:24.858 --> 00:02:27.160
Because the fireplace smoked, she opened a window.

36
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:32.890
She heard Adair enter his study, but she didn't hear anything else until Adair's mother and sister came

37
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.171
home around eleven-thirty."

38
00:02:35.071 --> 00:02:36.271
Lestrade said that when Mrs.

39
00:02:36.331 --> 00:02:39.993
Adair went to say good night to her son, she found his study door locked.

40
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:43.074
She called for a servant, who forced the door open.

41
00:02:43.794 --> 00:02:46.796
Adair had been shot to death, but there was no weapon in the room.

42
00:02:47.756 --> 00:02:51.818
On his desk were some piles of money and a list of club members' names and some numbers.

43
00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:56.259
"It sounds like Adair was trying to figure out how much money he had won and lost at

44
00:02:56.299 --> 00:02:57.420
cards," I said.

45
00:02:58.296 --> 00:03:00.477
"But why had he locked the door from the inside?"

46
00:03:02.477 --> 00:03:03.498
Lestrade shook his head.

47
00:03:03.518 --> 00:03:05.438
"That's one of the mysteries.

48
00:03:06.278 --> 00:03:08.879
It's unclear whether Adair or the murderer locked the door.

49
00:03:09.859 --> 00:03:13.320
One theory was that the murderer had climbed through the window and locked the door.

50
00:03:14.201 --> 00:03:18.862
But if you examine the scene, you'll see that there was no way anyone could access the window."

51
00:03:18.882 --> 00:03:22.083
"Park Lane is such a busy street," I said.

52
00:03:22.103 --> 00:03:24.844
"There are pedestrians on that street even at night.

53
00:03:25.876 --> 00:03:28.358
I'm surprised that no one saw the murderer or heard the shot."

54
00:03:28.378 --> 00:03:30.599
"That's another part of the mystery!"

55
00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:33.460
Lestrade threw up his hands in exasperation.

56
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.002
"If only Sherlock Holmes were here, he'd figure it out."

57
00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:41.545
The next evening I decided to stroll down Park Lane to examine the scene of the crime.

58
00:03:43.246 --> 00:03:47.148
A few people were looking up at a window while they listened to a man talking about his

59
00:03:47.188 --> 00:03:48.169
own theories on the case.

60
00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:51.531
"That must be the window of Adair's study," I thought.

61
00:03:51.551 --> 00:03:55.950
"And Lestrade was right—it's completely inaccessible from the ground.

62
00:03:56.831 --> 00:03:59.713
There's no tree or downspout that anyone could climb to reach it."

63
00:04:00.594 --> 00:04:04.417
I listened to the man talking about the case but quickly decided his theories were absurd.

64
00:04:05.097 --> 00:04:08.160
From his manner and his dress, I thought he might be a private detective.

65
00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:12.804
Turning away, I bumped into an elderly man who was standing behind me on the sidewalk.

66
00:04:12.824 --> 00:04:14.425
"Oh, excuse me!"

67
00:04:14.865 --> 00:04:17.648
I said, knocking some old books he was carrying to the ground.

68
00:04:18.869 --> 00:04:22.892
As I bent to pick them up, I noticed that one was The Origin of Tree Worship.

69
00:04:25.032 --> 00:04:28.433
Wondering if this fellow was a collector of used books, I tried to apologize.

70
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:32.514
But the old man snarled at me and hurried away with his precious volumes.

71
00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:37.696
I glanced up at Adair's window again, but feeling more puzzled than ever, I headed back home.

72
00:04:38.716 --> 00:04:41.937
I hadn't been sitting in my study five minutes when my maid announced a visitor.

73
00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:47.179
To my astonishment, it was the book collector carrying about a dozen volumes with one arm.

74
00:04:48.299 --> 00:04:51.600
"You're surprised to see me, sir," he said in an odd, croaking voice.

75
00:04:52.748 --> 00:04:55.490
His weather-beaten face peered out from a frame of white hair.

76
00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:56.810
I nodded.

77
00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.112
"May I ask how you knew where I lived?"

78
00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:05.555
"Well, I have a conscience, sir, and I thought I'd apologize to such a kind gentleman," he said.

79
00:05:05.575 --> 00:05:09.638
"I hobbled after you and saw you enter this house.

80
00:05:10.598 --> 00:05:14.160
If I was a bit gruff in my manner when we met earlier, I didn't mean any harm.

81
00:05:15.121 --> 00:05:17.122
I'm much obliged to you for picking up my books."

82
00:05:18.243 --> 00:05:20.424
"You're making too much of a small thing," I said.

83
00:05:21.292 --> 00:05:22.933
"Your books must be very important to you."

84
00:05:23.713 --> 00:05:24.433
"Yes, indeed!

85
00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:25.634
Books are my life."

86
00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:29.535
The old man's eyes lit up, and he put down his pile of treasures.

87
00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.477
I began to wonder how I was going to get this stranger out of my house.

88
00:05:33.497 --> 00:05:38.779
"You probably don't realize it, but I'm a neighbor of yours," he said, looking around the room.

89
00:05:38.799 --> 00:05:43.761
"You'll find my little bookshop at the corner of Church Street, and I'd be very happy to see

90
00:05:43.781 --> 00:05:44.181
you there.

91
00:05:46.962 --> 00:05:48.583
Maybe you collect books yourself, sir?"

92
00:05:49.463 --> 00:05:50.684
He pulled a book from his pile.

93
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.384
"Here's British Birds, for example, in fine condition and a bargain.

94
00:05:56.205 --> 00:05:57.166
As are all my other books!

95
00:05:58.347 --> 00:06:01.530
Five volumes would just fill up that empty space on the shelf over there."

96
00:06:02.491 --> 00:06:06.876
I turned my head to look at the bookcase, and when I looked back, Sherlock Holmes was standing

97
00:06:06.896 --> 00:06:07.897
there, smiling at me.

98
00:06:08.818 --> 00:06:11.100
I stared at him for a few seconds in utter amazement.

99
00:06:12.161 --> 00:06:14.544
Then, for the first time in my life, I fainted.
